(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["clinical-pathophysiology-i-exam-1-ent-pathophysiology-quiz-version-2"]={"title":"ENT Pathophysiology Quiz 2 — Clinical Pathophysiology I","category":"Clinical Pathophysiology I","exam":"Exam 1","sem":"fall-2026","questions":[{"q":"Which statement describes the source of anterior epistaxis?","choices":["Woodruff's plexus on the posterolateral wall, about 90 percent","Kiesselbach's plexus on the anterior septum, about 90 percent","The sphenopalatine artery, about 50 percent","The anterior ethmoidal artery, about 10 percent"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Anterior epistaxis, 90 percent of cases, comes from Kiesselbach's plexus on the anterior septum; the posterior 10 percent come from Woodruff's plexus on the posterolateral wall. — 5. ENT Clinical Pathophysiology_STUDENT VERSION.pptx, Slide 23"},{"q":"Why does cerumen impaction cause conductive rather than sensorineural loss?","choices":["It blocks the canal, so sound never reaches the conducting chain","It damages the outer hair cells","It stiffens the stapes footplate","It loads the tympanic membrane with fluid"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. The cochlea is untouched, and the defect is purely one of delivery. — 5. ENT Clinical Pathophysiology_STUDENT VERSION.pptx, Slide 9"},{"q":"Which pair of tuning fork findings indicates conductive hearing loss?","choices":["Weber to the AFFECTED ear; Rinne shows bone conduction greater than air","Weber to the UNAFFECTED ear; Rinne shows air conduction greater than bone","Weber to the affected ear; Rinne shows air conduction greater than bone","Weber is equal; Rinne shows bone greater than air bilaterally"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. With sound wave transmission to the oval window defective, Weber lateralizes to the AFFECTED ear and Rinne is abnormal, with bone conduction greater than air. — 5. ENT Clinical Pathophysiology_STUDENT VERSION.pptx, Slide 7"},{"q":"Which history most separates labyrinthitis from Meniere disease?","choices":["Unilateral rather than bilateral symptoms","The presence of tinnitus","A recent upper respiratory infection","The presence of sensorineural hearing loss"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Labyrinthitis is caused by viral infection, typically a recent viral upper respiratory infection, whereas Meniere disease arises from defective endolymph resorption causing hydrops. — 5. ENT Clinical Pathophysiology_STUDENT VERSION.pptx, Slide 17"},{"q":"Where do the pressure waves that begin hearing actually travel first?","choices":["The scala tympani","The cochlear duct itself","The scala vestibuli","The semicircular canals"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Stapes movement at the oval window starts them there, and they displace endolymph in the cochlear duct. — 5. ENT Clinical Pathophysiology_STUDENT VERSION.pptx, Slide 5"},{"q":"What protects the outer third of the ear canal?","choices":["Mucus from goblet cells","Ciliated epithelium sweeping debris outward","A keratin plug at the isthmus","Cerumen from ceruminous glands, and hair follicles"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Ceruminous glands produce protective cerumen, and the outer third of the canal also has protective hair follicles. — 5. ENT Clinical Pathophysiology_STUDENT VERSION.pptx, Slide 4"},{"q":"Which description fits vocal cord polyps, as distinct from nodules?","choices":["Bilateral fibrous calluses from chronic phonotrauma","Bilateral soft lesions at the anterior commissure from reflux","Unilateral, soft and pedunculated on the middle third, after one severe strain","Unilateral fibrous calluses from chronic yelling"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. One event rather than chronic repetition, and the healing response is inflammatory rather than a callus. Site and laterality both differ from nodules. — 5. ENT Clinical Pathophysiology_STUDENT VERSION.pptx, Slide 25"},{"q":"Which description fits allergic rhinitis?","choices":["IgG-mediated type 2 hypersensitivity with purulent rhinorrhea","Immune complex deposition, type 3 hypersensitivity","T cell mediated, type 4 delayed hypersensitivity","IgE-mediated type 1 hypersensitivity, with CLEAR rhinorrhea"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Type 1 hypersensitivity driven by IgE (immunoglobulin E) against inhaled allergens. The CLEAR discharge separates it from bacterial sinusitis, which is purulent. Allergic shiners complete the picture. — 5. ENT Clinical Pathophysiology_STUDENT VERSION.pptx, Slide 20"},{"q":"Which physical law explains why a small septal deviation causes disproportionate obstruction?","choices":["Fick's law of diffusion","Laplace's law","Boyle's law","Poiseuille's law"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. By Poiseuille's law even small airway narrowing dramatically increases resistance, so a modest deviation can produce unilateral resistance and chronic nasal obstruction. — 5. ENT Clinical Pathophysiology_STUDENT VERSION.pptx, Slide 22"},{"q":"A temporal bone fracture causes conductive loss by which mechanism?","choices":["Discontinuity of the ossicular chain","Stiffness of the ossicular chain","Mass loading of the tympanic membrane","Obstruction of the external canal"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. The chain is physically disrupted so the wave cannot be conducted across it. Ossicular necrosis produces the same end result by erosion rather than trauma. — 5. ENT Clinical Pathophysiology_STUDENT VERSION.pptx, Slide 9"},{"q":"Which causes produce CENTRAL vertigo?","choices":["Benign paroxysmal positional vertigo, Meniere disease and labyrinthitis","Brainstem stroke, multiple sclerosis and cerebellar tumor","Otosclerosis and cerumen impaction","Presbycusis and noise trauma"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. All involve the brainstem or cerebellum, which is why the nystagmus can be vertical and is not suppressed by fixation, and why other neurological signs appear. — 5. ENT Clinical Pathophysiology_STUDENT VERSION.pptx, Slide 15"},{"q":"Why does labyrinthitis impair hearing AND balance together?","choices":["Defective endolymph resorption ballooning the scala media","Dislodged otoconia striking structures in the endolymph","Fixation of the stapes footplate","Inflammation across both the semicircular canals and the cochlea"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Inflammatory swelling, vascular congestion and endolymphatic disruption affect the semicircular canals and the cochlea at once, impairing balance and the organ of Corti together. — 5. ENT Clinical Pathophysiology_STUDENT VERSION.pptx, Slide 17"},{"q":"Why are there three semicircular canals rather than one?","choices":["Each detects a different speed of rotation","Two detect rotation and one detects gravity","Redundancy in case one is damaged","They sit in three planes, so rotation in any direction is detected"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Sagittal, coronal and transverse between them cover all rotational movement. — 5. ENT Clinical Pathophysiology_STUDENT VERSION.pptx, Slide 14"},{"q":"What does it mean, structurally, for lymph nodes to be MATTED?","choices":["They have lost their individual capsules and move as one mass","They are tender and mobile","They are smaller than normal","They fluctuate in size with infection"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Matted nodes move together as one mass, and persistent rubbery or matted nodes in older adults require assessment to rule out lymphoma. — 5. ENT Clinical Pathophysiology_STUDENT VERSION.pptx, Slide 27"},{"q":"Which organisms most commonly cause bacterial otitis media?","choices":["Streptococcus pneumoniae, Haemophilus influenzae, Moraxella catarrhalis","Pseudomonas aeruginosa and Staphylococcus aureus","Aspergillus niger and Candida albicans","Group A Streptococcus alone"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. The classic middle ear trio. Viral causes are respiratory syncytial virus, rhinovirus, influenza and adenovirus. — 5. ENT Clinical Pathophysiology_STUDENT VERSION.pptx, Slide 13"},{"q":"Which description fits inferior turbinate hypertrophy?","choices":["Cartilage displacement off the midline; a congenital deformity","Venous engorgement, edema or bony hypertrophy; decongestant rebound","Eosinophilic polyp formation; an allergic reaction","Bacterial infection of the turbinate bone; osteomyelitis"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. The inferior turbinates enlarge from venous sinusoid engorgement, mucosal edema or bony hypertrophy; rebound hyperemia from topical decongestants is rhinitis medicamentosa. — 5. ENT Clinical Pathophysiology_STUDENT VERSION.pptx, Slide 21"},{"q":"What is the bone remodeling sequence in otosclerosis?","choices":["Osteoblastic overgrowth of dense cortical bone without resorption","Osteoclastic resorption, then spongy osteoid replacement","Demineralization of the ossicles from chronic inflammation","Fibrous scarring of the round window membrane"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Abnormal osteoclastic bone resorption is followed by hypervascular spongy osteoid replacement around the otic capsule and stapes footplate, which then fixes the footplate. — 5. ENT Clinical Pathophysiology_STUDENT VERSION.pptx, Slide 10"},{"q":"What is the sequence by which Eustachian tube dysfunction creates negative middle ear pressure?","choices":["The tube fails to open, so the mucosa absorbs the gases","Mucus production increases faster than the tube can drain it","Bacterial infection erodes the tube lining and opens it permanently","The tympanic membrane becomes stiff and pulls the ossicles inward"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. The middle ear is an air-filled space; if it is not re-ventilated, the mucosa absorbs the gases and the pressure falls. That negative pressure is the start of the otitis media sequence. — 5. ENT Clinical Pathophysiology_STUDENT VERSION.pptx, Slide 13"},{"q":"What is canalithiasis?","choices":["Calcification of the stapes footplate","Dislodged otoconia floating free in the semicircular canals","Accumulation of endolymph in the membranous labyrinth","Inflammatory debris within the cochlea after infection"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Canalithiasis means dislodged otoconia, the crystals, lying in the semicircular canals; the result is episodic vertigo, usually under a minute, with head position changes and no hearing loss. — 5. ENT Clinical Pathophysiology_STUDENT VERSION.pptx, Slide 18"},{"q":"Which structures detect linear acceleration and gravitational position?","choices":["The three semicircular canals","The otolith organs","The cupula and ampulla","The organ of Corti"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. They handle static and linear balance, while the semicircular canals handle rotation. The two systems together give the brain a complete picture of head position and movement. — 5. ENT Clinical Pathophysiology_STUDENT VERSION.pptx, Slide 14"},{"q":"What is the classic symptom tetrad of Meniere disease?","choices":["Continuous vertigo lasting days, unilateral tinnitus, unilateral loss, and nystagmus","Vertigo lasting under a minute on head movement, with no hearing loss","High-frequency hearing loss with tinnitus and no vertigo","Episodic vertigo for hours, low-frequency tinnitus, low-tone loss, aural fullness"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. All four, and the LOW-frequency emphasis is what distinguishes the hearing loss from presbycusis or noise damage, which take the high frequencies first. — 5. ENT Clinical Pathophysiology_STUDENT VERSION.pptx, Slide 16"},{"q":"Which feature of benign paroxysmal positional vertigo separates it most cleanly from the other peripheral causes?","choices":["The vertigo lasts several hours","It follows a viral upper respiratory infection","There is NO hearing loss","It produces aural fullness"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Meniere disease and labyrinthitis both take hearing with them; this does not, because the otoconia are a vestibular problem that never touches the cochlea. Attacks also last under a minute and follow head position. — 5. ENT Clinical Pathophysiology_STUDENT VERSION.pptx, Slide 18"},{"q":"Which mechanism explains the ototoxicity of gentamicin and tobramycin?","choices":["They cross-link DNA in the stria vascularis, disturbing endolymph ion homeostasis","Reactive oxygen species destroy outer hair cells from the base, so high pitches go first","They alter the stria vascularis potential reversibly","They inhibit the prestin motor protein in outer hair cells"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Aminoglycosides induce reactive oxygen species that selectively destroy outer hair cells, starting at the cochlear base, so high-pitch hearing is lost first. — 5. ENT Clinical Pathophysiology_STUDENT VERSION.pptx, Slide 11"},{"q":"Which description fits vocal cord nodules?","choices":["Unilateral pedunculated fluid-filled growths on the middle third, from a single severe strain","Bilateral wart-like exophytic lesions from viral infection","Unilateral firm masses with cord fixation, from malignancy","Bilateral symmetrical calluses at the anterior third junction, from chronic phonotrauma"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Chronic phonotrauma, the cords slamming together, causes basement membrane hyalinization, a callus. Nodules are bilateral and symmetrical at the anterior third junction. — 5. ENT Clinical Pathophysiology_STUDENT VERSION.pptx, Slide 25"},{"q":"Cerumen impaction, foreign bodies and canal exostoses all cause conductive loss by which of the four core mechanisms?","choices":["Obstruction","Mass loading","Stiffness effect","Discontinuity"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. A physical blockage stops sound penetrating down the canal. It is the first of four mechanisms, and the only one that acts before the sound reaches the drum. — 5. ENT Clinical Pathophysiology_STUDENT VERSION.pptx, Slide 9"},{"q":"Which time course fits the vertigo of labyrinthitis?","choices":["Sudden, lasting days, improving over weeks","Episodic, with attacks lasting several hours","Episodic, with attacks lasting under a minute","Continuous, permanent and non-improving"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Labyrinthitis vertigo is sudden and lasts days, improving over weeks, unlike the hours-long attacks of Meniere disease or the sub-minute attacks of positional vertigo. — 5. ENT Clinical Pathophysiology_STUDENT VERSION.pptx, Slide 17"},{"q":"In the organ of Corti, what physically opens the tip-link channels on the hair cells?","choices":["Direct pressure of endolymph on the hair cell body","Glutamate binding to the hair cell","Shearing of the stereocilia against the tectorial membrane","Stapes vibration transmitted directly to the hair cells"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Basilar membrane displacement bends the stereocilia against the rigid tectorial membrane, and that mechanical deflection is what opens the channels. Everything upstream exists to produce that shear. — 5. ENT Clinical Pathophysiology_STUDENT VERSION.pptx, Slide 5"},{"q":"Which features mark vertigo as PERIPHERAL rather than central?","choices":["Gradual onset, vertical nystagmus, non-suppressible, with neurological deficits","Gradual onset with hearing loss and tinnitus","Sudden onset, horizontal or rotational nystagmus, fatigable, suppressed by visual fixation","Absence of nystagmus entirely"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Peripheral vertigo involves the inner ear or cranial nerve VIII, with sudden onset and prominent horizontal or rotational nystagmus that is fatigable and suppressed by visual fixation. — 5. ENT Clinical Pathophysiology_STUDENT VERSION.pptx, Slide 15"},{"q":"Why does a deviated septum cause epistaxis and loss of smell?","choices":["Compression of the ethmoid arteries and the olfactory nerve","Turbulent airflow dries the mucosa; air misses the cribriform plate","Chronic infection eroding the septal vessels and olfactory epithelium","Autoimmune destruction of the septal cartilage"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Two separate consequences of the same disturbed airflow: local drying with fragile vessel breakdown on one hand, and failure to deliver odorant to the superior nasal vault on the other. — 5. ENT Clinical Pathophysiology_STUDENT VERSION.pptx, Slide 22"},{"q":"Why does a lesion of cranial nerve VIII produce the same audiometric pattern as cochlear damage?","choices":["Both stiffen the ossicles","Both obstruct the canal","Both sit beyond the conducting chain, so both give sensorineural loss","Both add mass to the tympanic membrane"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. The category is defined by where the defect sits, not by which of the two structures failed. — 5. ENT Clinical Pathophysiology_STUDENT VERSION.pptx, Slide 7"}]};
